<p>Buying Iberian ham looks simple until the same counter presents <strong>100% Iberian acorn-fed, Iberian acorn-fed, free-range feed-fed, feed-fed, 50%, 75%, black seal, red seal, green seal and white seal</strong>. These terms belong to the same legal system, but they do not describe the same thing. The most common mistake is to mix up two separate questions: <strong>how much Iberian breed ancestry the animal has</strong> and <strong>how it was fed and managed during the regulated finishing stage</strong>.</p>
<p>The clearest way to understand Spanish Iberian ham is to separate those two dimensions. Spain's Iberian Quality Standard, established by Royal Decree 4/2014, defines the commercial designations and the colour-coded seals used on hams and shoulders. Once you know the logic, the label becomes a useful technical summary rather than marketing jargon.</p>
<h2>The key idea: feeding and breed are different variables</h2>
<p><strong>Acorn-fed, free-range feed-fed and feed-fed</strong> describe feeding and management. <strong>100%, 75% or 50% Iberian</strong> describes breed percentage. A complete designation combines the two.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Information</th><th>What it tells you</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Feeding/management</td><td>How the pig was finished and managed</td><td>Acorn-fed, free-range feed-fed, feed-fed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Breed percentage</td><td>Proportion of Iberian genetics</td><td>100%, 75%, 50% Iberian</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seal colour</td><td>Quick legal category identifier</td><td>Black, red, green, white</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>This is why both a <strong>100% Iberian acorn-fed ham</strong> and a <strong>50% Iberian acorn-fed ham</strong> can exist. They share the acorn-fed finishing category but not the same breed composition.</p>
<h2>The four official seals</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Seal</th><th>Category</th><th>Feeding/management</th><th>Breed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Black</strong></td><td>Acorn-fed 100% Iberian</td><td>Montanera under the legal requirements</td><td>100% Iberian</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Red</strong></td><td>Acorn-fed Iberian</td><td>Acorn and natural dehesa resources during montanera</td><td>50% or 75% Iberian</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Green</strong></td><td>Free-range feed-fed Iberian</td><td>Feed plus outdoor/field management under the standard</td><td>Different permitted Iberian percentages</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>White</strong></td><td>Feed-fed Iberian</td><td>Mainly cereal and legume feed in regulated intensive systems</td><td>Different permitted Iberian percentages</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Our dedicated guide to the <a href="/en/black-red-green-white-seal-iberian-ham/">black, red, green and white Iberian ham seals</a> focuses on reading the colour system. Here the goal is broader: understanding what the production categories actually mean.</p>
<h2>What acorn-fed really means</h2>
<p>The term <strong>acorn-fed</strong> is not a decorative claim that can be added to any Iberian ham. Under the regulated system it identifies pigs that meet the conditions for the <em>montanera</em>, the traditional finishing period in the dehesa where animals forage on acorns, grass and other natural resources under defined requirements involving dates, age, weight gain and stocking conditions.</p>
<p>Montanera combines <strong>diet, movement and environment</strong>. It is not simply a pig being given a few acorns. The animal moves through the dehesa to forage and gains weight during this period, influencing the raw material that later enters the curing process.</p>
<p>There are two acorn-fed seals. <strong>Black</strong> adds the requirement of 100% Iberian breed. <strong>Red</strong> identifies acorn-fed Iberian ham from 50% or 75% Iberian animals.</p>
<h2>Is red-seal acorn-fed ham worse than black-seal ham?</h2>
<p>The black seal represents a narrower legal combination: <strong>acorn-fed plus 100% Iberian breed</strong>. The red seal shares the regulated acorn-fed finishing system but comes from crossbred animals. That distinction matters, but it does not allow you to predict every sensory detail of an individual ham.</p>
<p>Genetics are only one part of quality. Salting, piece weight, fat distribution, drying, cellar maturation and producer skill also shape the final result. Black identifies a more restrictive category; it does not replace good craftsmanship.</p>
<h2>What free-range feed-fed Iberian ham means</h2>
<p><strong>Free-range feed-fed Iberian ham</strong>, identified by a green seal, comes from animals fed mainly on cereal and legume feeds while being managed outdoors or in field conditions according to the standard. It is not simply “acorn-fed but with fewer acorns”. It is a different regulated production route.</p>
<p>For many buyers this category offers an attractive middle ground between flavour, fat development and price. But it should be understood on its own terms rather than presented as a lesser version of montanera.</p>
<p>The green seal does not by itself tell you whether the animal is 50%, 75% or 100% Iberian. Read the full sales designation for the breed percentage.</p>
<h2>What feed-fed Iberian ham means</h2>
<p><strong>Feed-fed Iberian ham</strong> carries a white seal. The pigs are fed mainly on formulated feeds in regulated intensive production. This category is generally the most accessible route into certified Iberian ham.</p>
<p>Feed-fed does not mean “bad ham”. It identifies a different production category. A skilled producer can make balanced, enjoyable feed-fed ham, while poor curing can compromise more expensive raw material. What changes is the production system and therefore the starting material, not the basic need for careful curing.</p>
<h2>What does 100% Iberian mean?</h2>
<p><strong>100% Iberian</strong> is reserved for products from animals whose parents meet the required pure Iberian genealogical conditions. It does not automatically mean acorn-fed. Breed percentage and feeding designation remain separate.</p>
<p>The useful buying question is therefore not “Is it 100% or acorn-fed?” but <strong>“What is the breed percentage, and what is the feeding category?”</strong>.</p>
<h2>What do 75% and 50% Iberian mean?</h2>
<p>When a product is not 100% Iberian, the percentage of Iberian breed must be stated as required by the standard. These percentages arise from regulated crosses, commonly involving Duroc on the non-Iberian side.</p>
<p>The percentage is not a score out of 100 for overall quality. It is <strong>breed information</strong>. A 50% Iberian acorn-fed ham is still legitimately acorn-fed; a 100% Iberian feed-fed ham is still feed-fed. Those examples show why both dimensions matter.</p>
<h2>What about “pata negra”?</h2>
<p>The expression <strong>pata negra</strong> has historically been used too loosely. Under the current standard, the protected use corresponds to <strong>acorn-fed 100% Iberian</strong>. A dark hoof by itself is not proof of category, because hoof colour is not a reliable substitute for traceability and labelling.</p>
<h2>Does “recebo” still exist as a current category?</h2>
<p><strong>Recebo is not one of the current designations</strong> under Royal Decree 4/2014. You may encounter it in older books, historic labels or conversations, but current certified Iberian products should be read using the present categories: acorn-fed, free-range feed-fed and feed-fed, combined with breed percentage.</p>
<h2>How taste and texture tend to differ</h2>
<p>Acorn-fed ham is commonly associated with particularly fluid fat, long aromatic persistence and a complex profile that reflects both the raw material and extended maturation. A strong free-range feed-fed ham can offer excellent juiciness and balance. Feed-fed ham may present a more direct profile at a lower price point.</p>
<p>These are tendencies, not guarantees. <strong>The seal does not cure the ham.</strong> Salt, temperature, humidity, maturation time, piece anatomy and producer decisions remain decisive. Two hams with the same seal colour can still taste noticeably different.</p>
<h2>Does acorn-fed always mean a longer curing time?</h2>
<p>No legal equation says “black seal equals 48 months” or “white seal equals 24 months”. Minimum processing periods are regulated separately, and real maturation depends on the piece. Our guide to <a href="/en/how-long-is-iberian-ham-cured-what-determines-curing-time/">how long Iberian ham is cured</a> explains that distinction in detail.</p>
<h2>How to read an Iberian ham label in 30 seconds</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify the cut:</strong> ham or shoulder.</li>
<li><strong>Find the feeding designation:</strong> acorn-fed, free-range feed-fed or feed-fed.</li>
<li><strong>Read the breed percentage:</strong> 100%, 75% or 50% Iberian where applicable.</li>
<li><strong>Check the seal colour:</strong> black, red, green or white.</li>
<li><strong>Then compare producer, weight and format.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Treat curing months as extra information</strong>, not as a substitute for the legal designation.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Which category should you choose?</h2>
<p>If the ham is the centrepiece of a celebration, a premium gift or a tasting where you want maximum depth, <strong>acorn-fed</strong> is the natural place to look. For frequent consumption with a strong balance between character and budget, <strong>free-range feed-fed</strong> can be compelling. For a more accessible introduction to certified Iberian ham, a well-made <strong>feed-fed</strong> piece can make perfect sense.</p>
<p>Within the chosen category, consider producer, weight and whether you want a whole piece or sliced packs. If you are deciding between the rear and front leg, see our guide to <a href="/en/iberian-ham-or-shoulder-differences-which-to-choose/">Iberian ham versus shoulder</a>.</p>
<h2>The definitive rule</h2>
<p>Remember one sentence: <strong>acorn-fed, free-range feed-fed and feed-fed describe feeding and management; 100%, 75% and 50% describe breed; black, red, green and white are the official seals that summarise the legal category</strong>.</p>
<p>Black is acorn-fed 100% Iberian. Red is acorn-fed Iberian at 50% or 75%. Green is free-range feed-fed Iberian. White is feed-fed Iberian. Once you understand this architecture, the price differences become easier to interpret and the word “Iberian” stops being an imprecise catch-all.</p>
